WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:03.110
- [Narrator] Imagine if you
could stick out your tongue

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:05.480
and lick your belly button.

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:08.890
Turns out that would be
an easy feat for a frog

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:11.810
if they actually had belly buttons.

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:15.040
A frog's tongue is one-third
of its body length,

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.103
and that's just the start of
this miraculous body part.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:24.728
That long tongue is covered
with thousands of mucus glands,

00:00:24.728 --> 00:00:28.550
which secrete some of the
stickiest spit on Earth.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.250
In fact, when researchers studied

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:33.270
horned frog saliva in 2014,

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.530
they found these frogs
could lift 1.4 times

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:39.680
their own body weight
with their sticky tongues.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.500
That's like a human lifting a refrigerator

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.240
with their tongue!

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:45.200
Because of this,

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.675
frogs don't go whipping
out their tongues for fun,

00:00:47.675 --> 00:00:49.470
but when they finally do,

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.490
it can grab a meal
straight out of the air.

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:54.880
You see, although frogs can't fly,

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.293
many hunt some of the fleetest,
most agile winged animals

00:00:58.293 --> 00:01:01.930
like flies, moths, and dragonflies.

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:03.490
They don't even have to chase their meals.

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:05.850
They just sit and wait.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.750
It's even more impressive
when you consider

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:11.864
that flies experience time
more slowly than other animals.

00:01:11.864 --> 00:01:15.230
Each second feels like
four seconds to a fly,

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:16.810
so they have all the time in the world

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:18.300
to escape a predator.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.090
Just think about the last
time you tried swatting a fly.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:22.940
Not so easy, right?

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.680
Well, frogs have a trick up their sleeve:

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.210
an ultra-fast tongue.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:30.670
Their tongue is made of
two powerful muscle groups:

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.330
an extender and a retractor.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.990
The extender fires the
tongue towards its prey

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:38.233
at an astounding 4 meters per second.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.510
At the same time, the
frog flicks its jaws open,

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.640
which rotates the tongue as it fires,

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.620
like a speeding bullet.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:47.453
Bam!

00:01:47.453 --> 00:01:50.240
Before you can blink, it's all over.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.780
Researchers found that
frogs can snatch their prey

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.420
in under .07 seconds,

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.763
five times faster than you can blink.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:01.560
But speed isn't a frog's only weapon.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.700
A frog's tongue is 10
times softer than ours,

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:07.430
about as pliable as your brain, in fact.

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:10.390
And this softness makes it super flexible

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:12.830
so it can wrap itself around its victim,

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:16.300
slathering the fly with
a super sticky saliva,

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:19.060
trapping it in place like glue.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.380
This fly is going nowhere.

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:24.340
All that's left to do is reel it in.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:27.080
The frog's retractor
muscle yanks on the tongue,

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:30.160
which zooms backwards like a bungee chord.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.920
Within 15/100s of a second,

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:35.650
it disappears back into the mouth.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:39.160
To dislodge its prize, the
frog sucks its eyeballs

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:40.930
back into its head.

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.730
That pressure slides
the prey off its tongue,

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:46.020
ready to be swallowed whole.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:46.853
Gulp!

00:02:47.418 --> 00:02:48.551
G-g-gulp!

